ich we  had seen the fellow take from his pocket  to wipe off the crimson stain  it had adhered to the blade when the  knife fell  the envelope fell too at least  i murmured as i stared at this grim  relic this is a singular coincidence  the blood upon the blade had dried it  required but little to cause the  envelope to fall away  as a matter of fact while i was still  holding the weapon in my hand it fell to  the floor  i picked it up it was addressed in a  woman's hand  francis joins esquire fairly streetham  i at once recognized the name as that of  a well-known owner of racehorses  and so-called gentlemen rider  not the least singular part of all that  singular story  was that the letter inside that envelope  which was afterwards opened and read by  the proper authorities  was from mr joins his wife it was a  loving tender letter from a wife who was  an invalid abroad  to a husband whom she supposed was  thinking of her at home  mr joins was never arrested and that for  this sufficient reason  that when the agents of the law arrived  at his residence mr joins  was dead he had committed suicide on the  very night on which we saw that  call it vision on board the water lily  i viewed the corpse against my will i  was not called in evidence  had i been i was prepared to swear as  was my wife  that mr joins was the man whom i had  seen in a dream that night  it was shown at the inquest that he had  suffered of late from horrid dreams  that he had scarcely dared to sleep i  wonder if  in that last and most awful of his  dreams he had seen my face  seen it as i saw his  it was afterwards shown from inquiries  which were made  that mr joins and mr bush tenon to the  self  were beyond all doubt one and the same  person  on the singular circumstances which  caused that discovery to be made  i offer no comment  you
 

